# VitalForge Project Rules - Ruby 3.2.2, Rails 8.0, PostgreSQL

## Technology Stack
- Ruby 3.2.2
- Rails 8.0.2
- PostgreSQL
- React + TypeScript + ESBuild (for frontend)
- ViewComponent (for React mounting)

## General Ruby & Rails Best Practices

### Ruby Style
- Use Ruby 3.2+ syntax and features
- Prefer keyword arguments over positional arguments
- Use safe navigation operator (&.) where appropriate
- Avoid monkey patching; use refinements if necessary
- Follow Rubocop Rails Omakase style guide
- Use meaningful variable and method names (no single letters except in blocks)
- Keep methods under 15 lines when possible

### Rails Conventions
- Follow REST conventions for routes and controllers
- Use Rails naming conventions (singular models, plural controllers)
- Keep controllers thin - business logic belongs in models or service objects
- Use concerns for shared behavior, but don't overuse them
- Prefer built-in Rails methods over custom implementations

## Database & PostgreSQL Best Practices

### Migrations
- ALWAYS make migrations reversible (provide `down` method or use reversible methods)
- Add NOT NULL constraints in migrations, not just validations
- Add foreign key constraints for referential integrity
- Create indexes for foreign keys and frequently queried columns
- Use `add_index` with descriptive names for complex indexes
- Add comments to migrations explaining WHY, not WHAT
- Never edit existing migrations that have been run in production
- Use database-level defaults where appropriate
- Test migrations both up and down before committing

### Database Design
- Use PostgreSQL-specific features (JSONB, arrays, full-text search) when beneficial
- Add CHECK constraints for data validation at database level
- Use partial indexes for conditional uniqueness
- Prefer integer IDs unless UUIDs are specifically needed
- Always add timestamps (created_at, updated_at) to tables
- Use proper data types (don't store dates as strings)
- Add database-level unique constraints, not just model validations

### Data Integrity
- ALWAYS use database transactions for multi-step operations
- Add foreign key constraints with appropriate ON DELETE behavior
- Use database-level validations AND model-level validations
- Prefer strong parameters over manual attribute whitelisting
- Validate presence, format, and uniqueness at both database and model levels
- Use `dependent: :destroy` or `dependent: :restrict_with_error` appropriately

## Model Best Practices

### ActiveRecord Models
- Keep models focused on domain logic, not HTTP concerns
- Use scopes for reusable queries
- Prefer `find_by` over `where.first`
- Use `find_each` or `in_batches` for large datasets
- Avoid N+1 queries - use `includes`, `eager_load`, or `preload`
- Use `has_secure_password` for password fields (never store plain text)
- Validate email formats with URI::MailTo::EMAIL_REGEXP
- Add model-level callbacks sparingly - prefer service objects for complex logic

### Validations
```ruby
# Good - specific and clear
validates :email, presence: true, 
                  uniqueness: { case_sensitive: false },
                  format: { with: URI::MailTo::EMAIL_REGEXP }

# Bad - too generic
validates :email, presence: true
```

### Associations
- Always specify `dependent:` option on has_many/has_one
- Use `inverse_of` for bidirectional associations
- Prefer `has_many :through` over `has_and_belongs_to_many`
- Use `optional: true` explicitly when belongs_to is not required

## Controller Best Practices

### Keep Controllers Thin
```ruby
# Good - delegate to model/service
def create
  @user = User.new(user_params)
  if @user.save
    redirect_to @user, notice: "User created"
  else
    render :new, status: :unprocessable_entity
  end
end

# Bad - business logic in controller
def create
  @user = User.new(user_params)
  @user.email = @user.email.downcase
  @user.setup_default_preferences
  if @user.save
    UserMailer.welcome(@user).deliver_later
    redirect_to @user, notice: "User created"
  else
    render :new, status: :unprocessable_entity
  end
end
```

### Controller Patterns
- Use strong parameters for all user input
- Return proper HTTP status codes (422 for validation errors, not 200)
- Use `before_action` for authentication/authorization
- Keep actions RESTful when possible
- Use `respond_to` blocks for multiple formats
- Never expose model IDs directly in URLs for sensitive resources

## Authentication & Security

### Authentication Rules
- ALWAYS use `has_secure_password` with bcrypt
- NEVER store passwords in plain text
- Use `reset_session` after successful login (prevent session fixation)
- Implement account lockout for failed login attempts
- Store session-sensitive data server-side, not in cookies
- Use `protect_from_forgery with: :exception` in ApplicationController
- Pass CSRF tokens to React components for API calls

### Security Checklist
- Sanitize user input (use strong parameters)
- Escape output (Rails does this by default in ERB)
- Use parameterized queries (ActiveRecord does this automatically)
- Enable HTTPS in production (`config.force_ssl = true`)
- Set secure session cookies (httponly: true, secure: true, same_site: :lax)
- Validate file uploads if accepting them
- Rate limit authentication endpoints

## React + Rails Integration

### Hybrid Architecture (Ryan Bigg Pattern)
- Use ERB for authentication pages (login, signup, password reset)
- Use React components for interactive app pages (dashboard, forms)
- Mount React components via ViewComponent
- Pass data from Rails to React through ViewComponent props
- Use Rails API endpoints for React component data fetching

### ViewComponent Pattern
```ruby
# Good - prepare data in Ruby, pass to React
class WorkoutTracker::Component < ReactComponent
  def initialize(user:)
    super("WorkoutTracker", raw_props: { user: user })
  end

  def props
    {
      userId: raw_props[:user].id,
      recentWorkouts: raw_props[:user].workouts.recent.map(&:to_json),
      csrfToken: helpers.form_authenticity_token
    }
  end
end
```

### API Endpoints for React
- Create dedicated API namespace (`/api/`) for React components
- Return JSON responses with proper error handling
- Include CSRF protection for non-GET requests
- Use serializers or Jbuilder for consistent JSON structure
- Return proper HTTP status codes

## Testing Best Practices

### Test Coverage
- Write tests for models (validations, methods, scopes)
- Test authentication flows (login, logout, signup)
- Test authorization (can users access what they should?)
- Test API endpoints that React components use
- Use fixtures or factories (FactoryBot) for test data

### Testing Patterns
- Use descriptive test names that explain behavior
- Follow AAA pattern: Arrange, Act, Assert
- Don't test Rails framework code
- Test edge cases and error conditions
- Use system tests for critical user flows

## Performance Best Practices

### Database Performance
- Add indexes for foreign keys automatically
- Add indexes for columns used in WHERE, ORDER BY, or JOIN
- Use `select` to load only needed columns
- Use `pluck` instead of `map` for single attributes
- Use counter caches for frequently accessed counts
- Consider database views for complex queries

### Caching
- Use fragment caching for expensive view renders
- Cache expensive calculations at model level
- Use Rails.cache for application-level caching
- Consider counter_cache for association counts

### N+1 Queries
```ruby
# Good - eager loading
@users = User.includes(:workouts).limit(10)

# Bad - N+1 query
@users = User.limit(10)
@users.each { |user| user.workouts.count }
```

## Code Organization

### DRY Principle
- Extract repeated code into methods, concerns, or service objects
- Use partials for repeated view code
- Use helpers for view logic
- Use decorators/presenters for complex view formatting
- Don't repeat validations - use shared concerns or inheritance

### Service Objects
- Use for complex business logic
- Use for multi-model operations
- Use for external API interactions
- Keep them focused on a single responsibility
- Place in `app/services/` directory

### File Structure
```
app/
  components/        # ViewComponents
  controllers/
    api/            # API endpoints for React
    concerns/       # Shared controller logic
  models/
    concerns/       # Shared model logic
  services/         # Business logic objects
  serializers/      # JSON serialization
  views/
    api/           # API JSON views
```

## Git & Version Control

### Commit Messages
- Use present tense ("Add feature" not "Added feature")
- First line under 50 characters
- Reference issue numbers when applicable
- Be descriptive about WHY, not just WHAT

### Branch Strategy
- Create feature branches from main
- Use descriptive branch names (feature/user-authentication)
- Keep commits atomic and focused
- Don't commit commented-out code
- Don't commit credentials or secrets

## Environment & Configuration

### Environment Variables
- Use .env files for local development (with dotenv-rails)
- Never commit .env files
- Document required env vars in README
- Use Rails credentials for secrets in production
- Set different values per environment

### Configuration
- Use Rails.application.config for app settings
- Use initializers for gem configuration
- Keep environment-specific config in config/environments/
- Use feature flags for gradual rollouts

## Error Handling

### Production Error Handling
- Use rescue_from in ApplicationController for common errors
- Log errors with sufficient context
- Show user-friendly error messages
- Never expose stack traces to users in production
- Use error tracking service (Sentry, Rollbar, etc.)

### Validation Errors
- Return meaningful error messages
- Use Rails I18n for error messages
- Return 422 status for validation errors
- Include field-specific errors in JSON responses

## Documentation

### Code Comments
- Comment WHY, not WHAT
- Document complex algorithms or business logic
- Add TODO comments with context for future work
- Keep comments up to date with code changes

### README Requirements
- Setup instructions
- Environment variable requirements
- How to run tests
- How to start the server
- Deployment instructions

## Rails 8 Specific

### Solid Queue (Background Jobs)
- Use for email sending, data processing
- Keep jobs idempotent
- Add retry logic with exponential backoff
- Log job failures

### Solid Cache
- Leverage for application caching
- Set appropriate TTLs
- Use cache versioning

### Importmap (JavaScript)
- Pin JavaScript dependencies in importmap.rb
- Use for Rails-specific JavaScript
- Use ESBuild for React/TypeScript code

## Common Patterns to Follow

### Authentication Flow
1. User submits credentials
2. Controller validates and checks account status
3. Use reset_session to prevent session fixation
4. Create session with user ID
5. Redirect to dashboard

### Form Handling
1. Display form (GET)
2. Submit form (POST/PATCH)
3. Validate with strong parameters
4. Save or return errors with 422 status
5. Redirect on success, render on failure

### API Response Format
```ruby
# Success
{ data: { user: { id: 1, name: "John" } } }

# Error
{ errors: { email: ["can't be blank"] } }
```

## Anti-Patterns to Avoid

### Don't Do This
- ❌ Skip database constraints relying only on validations
- ❌ Put business logic in controllers
- ❌ Use callbacks for complex workflows (use services)
- ❌ Return 200 status for validation errors
- ❌ Rescue Exception (rescue specific errors)
- ❌ Use string interpolation in SQL queries
- ❌ Ignore N+1 query warnings
- ❌ Store sensitive data in sessions
- ❌ Skip authentication on API endpoints
- ❌ Use update_attribute (skips validations)

## Deployment Checklist

### Before Deploying
- [ ] All tests pass
- [ ] No pending migrations
- [ ] Environment variables configured
- [ ] Database backups in place
- [ ] SSL/HTTPS enabled
- [ ] Error tracking configured
- [ ] Logging configured
- [ ] Performance monitoring enabled

## Project-Specific Rules

### VitalForge Fitness App
- User authentication uses has_secure_password (no Devise)
- Session-based auth (not JWT)
- React components for workout tracking UI
- ERB for authentication pages
- PostgreSQL for data storage
- Use strong parameters for all workout/exercise data
- Validate exercise data before saving
- Track user fitness metrics with proper data types

## CSS & Styling Best Practices

### VitalForge Color Palette
- ALWAYS use CSS custom properties defined in `:root`
- Reference: See `.cursor/rules/frontend/frontend-rules.mdc` for complete palette
- Primary colors: Electric Blue (#2563EB), Energetic Orange (#F97316)
- Use `var(--electric-blue)` instead of hardcoded hex values
- Maintain consistent color usage across all pages

### CSS Structure
- Keep all styles in `app/assets/stylesheets/application.css`
- Use CSS custom properties for theming and consistency
- Follow BEM-like naming for complex components
- Group related styles together with comments
- Mobile-first responsive design

### Button Styling
```css
/* Primary actions - Orange gradient */
.btn-primary: Energetic orange, main CTAs, high-priority actions

/* Secondary actions - Blue gradient */
.btn-secondary: Electric blue, supporting actions

/* Destructive actions - Red outline/gradient */
.btn-outline: Bright red, delete/cancel actions
```

### Form Styling
- Light background by default (var(--warm-gray))
- White background on hover
- Blue border with glow on focus
- 2px borders for clarity
- Smooth transitions (0.3s ease)

### Card & Component Styling
- Use glassmorphism for overlay cards: `backdrop-filter: blur(20px)`
- Shadow system: Small (10px), Medium (20px), Large (40px)
- Border radius: Small (8px), Medium (12px), Large (20px)
- Always include hover states with transform

### Animation Guidelines
- Standard transitions: 300ms cubic-bezier(0.4, 0, 0.2, 1)
- Hover transforms: `translateY(-2px)` for lift effect
- Active transforms: `scale(0.98)` for press effect
- Use hardware-accelerated properties (transform, opacity)
- Keep animations subtle and purposeful

### Accessibility in Styles
- Maintain WCAG 2.1 AA contrast ratios minimum
- Focus indicators must be visible (blue glow)
- Touch targets minimum 44x44px
- Provide visual feedback for all interactions
- Test with color blindness simulators

## Documentation Requirements

### README Documentation
- MUST maintain comprehensive README.md in project root
- Include: Setup instructions, tech stack, features, API docs
- Update README when adding major features or changing architecture
- Include environment variable requirements
- Document all external dependencies

### Code Documentation Rules

#### When to Comment
```ruby
# ✅ GOOD - Explains WHY
# Lock account after 5 failed attempts to prevent brute force attacks
MAX_LOGIN_ATTEMPTS = 5

# ❌ BAD - Explains WHAT (obvious from code)
# Set max login attempts to 5
MAX_LOGIN_ATTEMPTS = 5
```

#### Required Documentation
- **Complex algorithms**: Document the approach and reasoning
- **Business logic**: Explain business rules and edge cases
- **Security decisions**: Document why security measures were chosen
- **Performance optimizations**: Explain what was optimized and why
- **Workarounds**: Document temporary solutions and plan for fixing

#### Method Documentation
```ruby
# Document public API methods
# @param user [User] The user to authenticate
# @param password [String] Plain text password
# @return [Boolean] true if authentication succeeds
# @raise [AccountLockedError] if account is locked
def authenticate_user(user:, password:)
  # Implementation
end
```

### File-Specific Documentation

#### Migration Files
```ruby
# ALWAYS add a comment explaining the purpose
class AddIndexToUsersEmail < ActiveRecord::Migration[8.0]
  # Adding index to improve email lookup performance in authentication
  # Email is queried on every login attempt
  def change
    add_index :users, :email, unique: true
  end
end
```

#### Service Objects
```ruby
# MUST include class-level documentation
# Handles workout creation with associated exercises
# 
# @example
#   WorkoutCreator.new(user, workout_params).call
#
# @raises [ActiveRecord::RecordInvalid] if validation fails
class WorkoutCreator
  # Implementation
end
```

### Documentation Files to Maintain

#### Required Documentation Files
1. **README.md** - Project overview, setup, usage
2. **DEVELOPMENT.md** - Development workflow, commands, troubleshooting
3. **MIGRATIONS_GUIDE.md** - Database migration patterns and examples
4. **STYLING_UPDATE.md** - Design system and color palette usage
5. **.cursorrules** - Project-specific coding standards

#### When to Create New Documentation
- New major feature → Add section to README
- New design pattern → Update relevant guide
- Breaking changes → Update DEVELOPMENT.md
- Architecture changes → Document in README
- Complex workflows → Create dedicated guide

### Changelog & Version Tracking
- Document significant changes in git commit messages
- Use meaningful commit messages: "Add user authentication with bcrypt"
- Not: "Update files" or "Fix bug"
- Consider maintaining CHANGELOG.md for major releases

## Project File Structure & Organization

### Standard Directory Structure
```
app/
  ├── assets/stylesheets/     # CSS files with color palette
  ├── components/             # ViewComponents for React mounting
  ├── controllers/
  │   ├── api/               # JSON API endpoints for React
  │   └── concerns/          # Shared controller behavior
  ├── models/
  │   └── concerns/          # Shared model behavior
  ├── services/              # Business logic objects
  ├── serializers/           # JSON serialization
  └── views/
      ├── api/               # JSON views (Jbuilder)
      └── layouts/           # Application layouts

config/
  └── initializers/          # App configuration

db/
  └── migrate/               # Database migrations (timestamped)

.cursor/
  └── rules/
      ├── frontend/          # Frontend-specific rules (color palette)
      └── backend/           # Backend-specific rules
```

### File Naming Conventions
- Models: `user.rb` (singular, snake_case)
- Controllers: `users_controller.rb` (plural, snake_case)
- Views: `users/index.html.erb` (plural directory)
- Services: `workout_creator.rb` (singular, snake_case)
- Components: `workout_tracker/component.rb`
- Migrations: `YYYYMMDDHHMMSS_create_users.rb`

### Where to Put New Code

#### Business Logic → Service Objects
```ruby
# app/services/workout_creator.rb
class WorkoutCreator
  def call
    # Complex workout creation logic
  end
end
```

#### View Logic → Helpers or Decorators
```ruby
# app/helpers/workout_helper.rb
module WorkoutHelper
  def format_workout_duration(seconds)
    # Formatting logic
  end
end
```

#### Shared Model Behavior → Concerns
```ruby
# app/models/concerns/trackable.rb
module Trackable
  extend ActiveSupport::Concern
  # Shared tracking logic
end
```

#### React Integration → ViewComponents
```ruby
# app/components/workout_tracker/component.rb
class WorkoutTracker::Component < ReactComponent
  # Props preparation for React
end
```

## Remember
- Security first - never skip authentication/authorization
- Data integrity - use database constraints
- Performance matters - watch for N+1 queries
- Test your code - especially authentication flows
- **Document your decisions** - explain WHY, not WHAT
- **Update documentation** when changing features
- **Follow the color palette** - use CSS custom properties
- **Maintain file structure** - put code in the right place
- Keep it simple - don't over-engineer
- Follow Rails conventions - don't fight the framework

